#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064064 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44341f33-035e-5e4a-9955-2590a9770f3a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Logarithms Using the Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "4b2df191-5a85-5600-b055-63d8a84ddcc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3983" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\log_{2} \\left( 2^{5} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The idea is that the log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=2$ and $x=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left( 2^{5}\\right) = 5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064065 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdcbbc8b-3510-5b4a-913b-5b69f997d434" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Special Values of Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "1c2d7a63-4d9c-55a5-809a-5c1f84531a6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3984" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\log_{8}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the general rule that\n\n$$\n\\log_n n = 1.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{8}{8} =1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064066 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a0909f-afba-56f6-a5a0-082403b04be3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Logarithm of a Fraction or Root"}] :question/id #uuid "8310cf45-a98c-589a-8df6-b707171095b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3985" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\log_{2} \\left(\\dfrac 1 {8} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ as a power of $2$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{1}{2^3} = 2^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{2} \\left(\\dfrac 1 {8} \\right)= \\log_{2} (2^{-3}).\n$$\n\nNow we can proceed as usual. The log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=2$ and $x=-3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}( 2^{-3} )= -3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064067 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1c6931-cc04-5014-9bf6-5ebc5dc07728" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Logarithms Using Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "d137aa5c-f148-5a6f-bbda-0d88557f8ea9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3987" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\log_{3}{9}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $9$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n9 = 3^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{3}{9} = \\log_{3}(3^2).\n$$\n\nNow we can proceed as usual. The log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b = 3$ and $x = 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{3}(3^2) = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44341f33-035e-5e4a-9955-2590a9770f3a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Logarithms Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd14dfe-60bb-58a0-bd26-51dc968d9c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517519} {:answer/id #uuid "22541458-e554-52ec-8376-f299f409d8cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517552} {:answer/id #uuid "6bba5b70-c8d5-58be-9d37-6faff7852146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517690} {:answer/id #uuid "86f365f9-48ea-58f6-a215-e979a92b0ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517741} {:answer/id #uuid "e4207623-dab0-5d9b-b03e-704090e79618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :an